  The narrative below was written for the ordered layout at the default settings and
  does not describe this run; read the tables above instead.

  Removing an end of an ordered sibling axis inverts the posterior at every separation
  tested, and the inversion deepens as the separation grows, which is what the geometric
  argument predicts: the wider the axis, the more decisively the removed end projects
  onto its single nearest retained neighbour while the retained classes keep splitting
  mass with neighbours on both sides.

  Removing an interior sibling never inverts, because the retained siblings lie on both
  sides and split the posterior.

  The family condition needs care and does not support a blanket claim. It does fall
  below one half at the smallest separations, but only where the retained classes are
  themselves unidentifiable, with closed accuracy between 0.36 and 0.75. That is a
  different defect: when training classes cannot be told apart, known artifacts are
  scored as unconfidently as novel ones and the comparison carries no information about
  nesting. Once the retained classes are identifiable the family condition recovers, and
  under a fitted forest it reaches 0.9999 at every separation, which is the behaviour the
  measured configuration C shows.

  The maximum unnormalised score never inverts in the edge condition, rising from 0.5188
  to 1.0000 across the sweep. Softmax discards the absolute scale and keeps only relative
  proximity, which is the quantity nesting corrupts; the unnormalised score keeps the
  scale and survives. That is the mechanism behind the measured observation that logit
  based scorers partially escape the inversion while posterior based scorers do not.

  None of this involves obfuscation, a feature extractor, a real program, or a fitted
  estimator, since the headline scorer is the Bayes posterior computed from the
  generating parameters. The inversion is therefore a consequence of the evaluation
  design and not a finding about obfuscation.

